  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 358 AM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nHigh pressure will bring dry conditions and near normal \ntemperatures in the 60s to lower 70s today, although northwest \nflow aloft could bring some mid to high clouds at times. Some \nlow RH values may be found across the west today, although light\nwinds are forecast today limiting any fire weather concerns.\nSurface high then moves east tonight with a steady southerly \nflow building behind it. This could continue mid to high \nclouds, while keeping low temperatures mild and generally in the\n40s. Surface low then moves through for Monday lifting a warm \nfront across the area, followed by a weak cold front later in \nthe day through Monday night. With abundant dry air associated \nwith this disturbance, mainly dry conditions are expected. The \nwarm front will bring above normal temperatures in the 70s to \nperhaps low to mid 80s. ECMWF EFI values for MaxT also starting \nto pick up on this above normal trend especially in western \nportions. With this warm and dry air, afternoon humidity values \nin the teens and 20s are expected across the west, and 40s east.\nBreezy southerly winds will be found for most areas, although \nespecially in central and eastern portions. The mentioned dry \nfront could bring a breezy westerly wind to western portions. \nThis shift of wind combined with low RH values could bring some \nincreased fire weather concerns to the west, and perhaps some \nnear critical fire weather conditions to the far southwest. Most\nareas will then see a shift to northwest winds Monday night, \nwhile temperatures remain mild and generally in the 40s to lower\n50s. Weak and mainly dry cold front stalls across the state on \nTuesday, with northwest flow aloft. The result will be slightly \ncooler yet still above normal temperatures. Breezy northwest \nwinds could linger, mainly in the north and east. Afternoon \nhumidity values in the 20s and 30s are also expected. Depending \non winds and low RH values, some increased fire weather concerns\nto perhaps isolated near critical fire weather conditions will \nalso be possible Tuesday afternoon and early evening, especially\nin the southwest. Surface high then moves in Tuesday night \nthrough Wednesday bringing light winds, dry conditions, and \ncontinued above normal temperatures. Cooler lows could then \nreturn Wednesday night, with lows forecast in the mid 30s to mid\n40s. \n\nMainly dry and continued above normal temperatures then look to\nremain through the end of the work week as a ridge continues to\ninfluence the area. Temperature spreads begin to grow early next\nweekend as timing of when/if the ridge breaks down remains an \narea of uncertainty. Some clusters try to push the next large\ntrough across the area through the weekend while others keep a\nblocking ridge. The NBM temperatures trends overall start to\ncool by late in the weekend indicating perhaps this next trough\nmoves through during the weekend or early the following week.\nThis trough pattern could also be some breezy winds for the \nupcoming weekend. If the trough pattern does progress across the\narea, chances for precipitation will also return. Current \nforecast does bring low to medium chances of showers Saturday \nnight into Sunday as a result of this potential pattern change.",
